      "seed": 6409,
      "response": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that the angles of a trapezoid form an arithmetic sequence. Let the four angles of the trapezoid be \\(a\\), \\(a+d\\), \\(a+2d\\), and \\(a+3d\\), where \\(a\\) is the smallest angle and \\(d\\) is the common difference of the arithmetic sequence. We know from the problem statement that the smallest angle \\(a = 60^\\circ\\).\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always \\(360^\\circ\\), we can write the equation for the sum of the angles as:\n\\[\na + (a+d) + (a+2d) + (a+3d) = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 60^\\circ\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n60^\\circ + (60^\\circ + d) + (60^\\circ + 2d) + (60^\\circ + 3d) = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we have:\n\\[\n60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + d + 2d + 3d = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n240^\\circ + 6d = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nTo find \\(d\\), we subtract \\(240^\\circ\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n6d = 120^\\circ\n\\]\nThen, we divide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nd = 20^\\circ\n\\]\nNow that we have the common difference \\(d = 20^\\circ\\), we can find the measure of the largest angle, which is \\(a + 3d\\):\n\\[\na + 3d = 60^\\circ + 3 \\cdot 20^\\circ = 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
      "answer": "120",
